  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\nIssued at 1214 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nA transient ridge is centered along the Mississippi River Valley\ntoday and will de-amplify considerably over the next 24 hours as\nit shifts along the East Coast. Despite the de-amplification of\nthe overall longwave ridge, thicknesses will build slightly\n(~2 dam) over the southeastern US. This feature will extend into\nthe lower and middle Mississippi River Valley. Along with\nincreasing moisture over the Mid-South, these factors will boost\nheat indices higher tomorrow, necessitating the expansion of the\ncurrent Heat Advisory. Low 70s dew points should hang on a bit\nlonger in northeast Mississippi, keeping it below advisory\ncriteria (greater than 105 F), but the remainder of the Mid- \nSouth can expect afternoon heat indices to peak between 106 and \n110 degrees. Guidance dew points, namely the NBM, have been a bit \nhigh, especially along and west of the Mississippi River. For \nconsistency's sake, shaved a degree or two from this guidance, \nkeeping heat indices below warning criteria (above 110 F). The \nridge will continue to suppress any convection this afternoon and \ntomorrow, resulting in dry conditions across the region.\n\nThursday has the potential to be just as hot as Wednesday,\nespecially along and south of I-40. The ridge will continue to\nbreak down on Thursday. Lowering thicknesses and increasing cloud\ncover late in the day over northeast Arkansas, the Missouri\nBootheel, and northwest Tennessee should keep temperatures below\nadvisory criteria (above 105 F). Another Advisory may be needed \nfarther south, including north Mississippi, east-central Arkansas,\nand portions of west Tennessee.\n\nThe potential for strong to severe thunderstorms Thursday appears\nto be minimal at this time, mainly due to weak shear. However,\nany storms that manage to develop will have the potential to\nproduce strong winds. Unfortunately, rainfall totals associated\nwith the front also appear minimal. WPC's rainfall totals suggest\nany portion of the Mid-South will be lucky to receive one-quarter\nof an inch Thursday night through Friday.\n\nFriday should be cooler with scattered to numerous showers and\nthunderstorms.  High temperatures are expected to be in the low\nto middle 90s. A weak trough will shift east of the area late\nFriday.  Expect to see a drier, warmer pattern return over the\nweekend as a weak ridge builds over the Plains then shifts east.",
